      "claim": "A ketoprofen-glucose prodrug crossed the BBB via transporter-mediated uptake in situ, indicating that glucose-containing molecules can be actively transported into the brain.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "Ginsenoside Rh3, a glycoside, accumulated to 520 ng/g in rat hippocampus after oral administration, showing meaningful brain exposure for glycosidic natural products.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a phenylethanoid glycoside with multiple sugars and catechol groups. All retrieved evidence from similar large, polar glycosides (HSYA, hesperidin, ginsenosides, glucose prodrugs) demonstrates measurable brain penetration after systemic administration, supporting a pass prediction for meaningful CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental record for the exact query molecule (acteoside-like) is present; prediction relies on structural analogues.",
    "Brain concentrations in the evidence are often total tissue levels, not unbound brain concentrations, but the task threshold accepts measurable CNS access.",
    "Potential efflux transporter liability of the query is not directly assessed, but the evidence indicates that efflux signals do not preclude meaningful access."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

